<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamada Nagamasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1590–1630</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamada Nagamasa was a Japanese adventurer who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in the early seventeenth century, led the Japanese community and its volunteer soldiers in the service of the Siamese kings, and won high rank at court. Drawn into the succession struggles that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasat-thong">Prasat Thong</a> to power, he was sent to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a> in the south, where he died in 1630, reportedly poisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamada-nagamasa.jpg" alt="Yamada Nagamasa" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamada-Nagamasa-Portrait-Shizuoka-Sengen-Shrine.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
